  "slug": "atlas-brain-reveals-specialized-f062",
  "summary": "Field-theoretic, operator-spectral, and critical-scaling methods are now applied directly to clinical and biochemical data as physics-constrained reductions.",
  "synthesis": "The common thread is constraining reduced descriptions with the governing structure of an explicit physical or dynamical model rather than fitting observations alone. A stochastic PK-PD field theory is fit to 1461 patient lesion records to predict noise-driven tumor relapse after high-dose chemotherapy. A Markovian transfer-operator model of metastatic dissemination is analyzed spectrally and matches the clinical organ-involvement hierarchy. A percolation-inspired analysis identifies complement opsonization on nanoparticles as a critical transition with universal scaling, limited by surface diffusion. The formalisms differ, but each derives tractable low-dimensional behavior from an explicit model. What is new is the application of field-theoretic, operator-spectral, and critical-scaling frameworks as physics-constrained reductions directly to clinical and biochemical data. All three items are early-stage preprints, so the evidence base remains limited.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-08-21"
}
